In `DecompressWithConfig` (middleware/decompress.go), the handler does:

```go
i := pool.Get()
gr, ok := i.(*gzip.Reader)
if !ok {
    return echo.NewHTTPError(http.StatusInternalServerError, i.(error).Error())
}
```

So if the pooled value is not a `*gzip.Reader`, it returns a 500 HTTP error (`echo.NewHTTPError(http.StatusInternalServerError, ...)`) whose message is the error string of the pooled value. Caveat: the fallback itself does an unchecked assertion `i.(error)`, so if the pooled value is neither a `*gzip.Reader` nor an `error`, that line panics rather than returning.

In middleware/body_limit.go, `limitedReader.Read` (lines 92-99) is:

```go
func (r *limitedReader) Read(b []byte) (n int, err error) {
	n, err = r.reader.Read(b)
	r.read += int64(n)
	if r.read > r.limit {
		return n, echo.ErrStatusRequestEntityTooLarge
	}
	return
}
```

So when the cumulative bytes read exceed the limit, it returns the byte count `n` just read together with `echo.ErrStatusRequestEntityTooLarge` (HTTP 413, defined in echo.go as `NewHTTPError(http.StatusRequestEntityTooLarge)`). Otherwise it returns the underlying reader's `n, err`. Note the check is strictly `>` (not `>=`).

In middleware/method_override.go, `MethodFromQuery` (lines 86-92) returns a `MethodOverrideGetter` closure:

```go
func MethodFromQuery(param string) MethodOverrideGetter {
	return func(c echo.Context) string {
		return c.QueryParam(param)
	}
}
```

So it returns a getter function that reads the overridden method from the given query parameter via `c.QueryParam(param)`.

In middleware/logger.go, the `bytes_in` tag (lines 187-192) is computed as:

```go
case "bytes_in":
	cl := req.Header.Get(echo.HeaderContentLength)
	if cl == "" {
		cl = "0"
	}
	return buf.WriteString(cl)
```

So when the request has no Content-Length header, it writes the literal string `0` (not the actual body length). It only reads the header — no `req.ContentLength` field, no byte counting. (Contrast with `bytes_out`, which uses the actual `res.Size`.)
